 "co_shanahan_crag": {
  "_expect": {
   "name": "Shanahan Crag"
  },
  "blurb": "Shanahan Crag is a set of Flatiron slabs and faces in the foothills above Boulder, just south of the more famous Slab. Most of its 28 climbs are traditional lines, with a handful of bolted routes and one top-rope. Grades stretch from easy fifth-class ridge scrambling through a solid block of 5.7 to 5.9 and on into the 5.11 and 5.12 range, so both gentle multi-pitch outings and steeper tests are on offer. The rock is solid conglomerate with huecos and cracks. The main faces point south and soak up sun through the middle of the day while staying sheltered from wind, though belays are exposed with little shade. Spring and fall suit it best, and it sees far fewer parties than the bigger Flatirons.",
  "review": {
   "sources": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatirons",
